 <p>For example, a title containing <code>Atomic updates</code> can match this query. The <code>0.8</code> weight reduces that alternative’s score contribution. This block only creates the query; it does not create an engine or print results.</p>
-<p>A prefix needs at least two normalized Unicode code points. Raveldex finds every matching complete term in the dictionary; if expansion exceeds a limit, the search rejects rather than silently dropping terms.</p>
+<p>A prefix needs at least two normalized Unicode code points. Raveldex finds every matching complete term in the dictionary; if expansion exceeds a limit, the search rejects rather than silently dropping terms. Ordinary lexical prefix lookup seeks an ordered live-term index. Near expansion caps it can retain a full dictionary scan to preserve exact error precedence. The ordered index adds one node per live field term and charged maintenance/restore work; existing work and deadline bounds still apply.</p>
 <p>Choose <a href="/raveldex/reference/typo/">typo tolerance</a>, <a href="/raveldex/reference/synonyms/">synonyms</a>, <a href="/raveldex/reference/literal/">literal/infix matching</a>, <a href="/raveldex/reference/proximity/">ordered proximity</a> or <a href="/raveldex/reference/query-syntax/">query syntax</a> explicitly when you need those behaviors. A plain string does not turn them on.</p>
 <h2 id="analysis-and-ranking">Analysis and ranking</h2>
 <p>The default analyzer, <code>basic-v1</code>, finds words and numbers with boundaries equivalent to <code>/[\p{L}\p{N}][\p{L}\p{N}\p{M}]*/gu</code>. It applies NFKC normalization and locale-independent lowercase once to each original token. It does not split the normalized result again.</p>
